  "raw_meta_yaml": "context:\n  version: \"0.3.0\"\n\npackage:\n  name: magicjar\n  version: ${{ version }}\n\nsource:\n  url: https://github.com/clintval/magicjar/archive/refs/tags/${{ version }}.tar.gz\n  sha256: f61dbbbd8598bfa9d5dbf6960aeb41846fc082a588c8a3f931b6a9af44737d7d\n\nbuild:\n  number: 0\n  # magicjar writes a POSIX shell preamble and marks the result executable, so\n  # it only makes sense on Unix.\n  skip:\n    - win\n  script:\n    env:\n      CARGO_PROFILE_RELEASE_STRIP: symbols\n      CARGO_PROFILE_RELEASE_LTO: fat\n    content:\n      - cargo auditable install --locked --no-track --bins --root ${{ PREFIX }} --path .\n      - cargo-bundle-licenses --format yaml --output ./THIRDPARTY.yml\n\nrequirements:\n  build:\n    - ${{ stdlib('c') }}\n    - ${{ compiler('c') }}\n    - ${{ compiler('rust') }}\n    - cargo-bundle-licenses\n    - cargo-auditable\n\ntests:\n  - script:\n      - magicjar --help\n  - package_contents:\n      bin:\n        - magicjar\n      strict: true\n\nabout:\n  homepage: https://github.com/clintval/magicjar\n  summary: Make a Java JAR self-executing by prepending a shell preamble.\n  description: |\n    magicjar turns a Java JAR into a single self-executing file. The input may\n    be a JAR, a symlink to one, a conda/bioconda wrapper script, or a shell\n    alias; magicjar resolves any of these down to the underlying JAR, prepends\n    a portable shell preamble, and writes one executable file. At runtime, JVM\n    flags are routed to the JVM and everything else passes through to the\n    program.\n  license: MIT\n  license_file:\n    - LICENSE\n    - THIRDPARTY.yml\n  repository: https://github.com/clintval/magicjar\n\nextra:\n  recipe-maintainers:\n    - clintval\n",
